==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*skrīþaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*(s)kreyt-}}, {{term/t|ine-pro|*(s)ker-||twist, turn, bend}}.  Germanic cognates include Old Saxon {{term/t|osx|skrīthan}}, Middle Dutch {{term/t|dum|schriden}} (Dutch {{term/t|nl|schrijden}}), Old High German {{term/t|goh|skrītan}} (German {{term/t|de|schreiten}}), Old Norse {{term/t|non|skríða}} (Swedish {{term/t|sv|skrida}}).  The Indo-European root also gave Latin {{term/t|la|crīsō|crīsāre}} and Middle Irish {{term|crith|lang=mga}} (Breton {{term/t|br|skrija||tremble with fear}}).

